Sure! Here are five examples of using printf specifiers.

```cpp
    char const* my_string = foobar; 
    // print a signed integer.
    printf(Test %d\n, 5);
    // print a string alongside it's address.
    printf(There is a string living in %p, it says \%s\\n, my_string, my_string);
    // print a signed integer as unsigned.
    printf(The number %u is printed incorrectly.\n, -10);
    // print a % character and a floating point number.
    printf(We can use %%f to print a floating point, such as %f.\n, 10.5);
    // print using the hexadecimal representation.
    printf(The number in hex is 0x%X, 57005);
```

You can use the following class in CSS to center a div:
```css
.center {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```
Apply it to the div you want to center using:
```html
<div class=center></div>
```

As an AI language model, I do not have access to your specific Excel add-in or its trial period validation mechanism. However, I can provide you with general guidelines on how to validate a trial period in C#:

Store the installation date of your software in a configuration file or database.
Calculate the number of days that have elapsed since the installation date using the current date.
Compare the number of days elapsed to the trial period duration.
If the number of days elapsed is greater than or equal to the trial period duration, disable the functionality of the software or prompt the user to purchase a license.
Here is an example C# code snippet that demonstrates this approach:

DateTime installationDate = GetInstallationDateFromConfig();
int trialPeriodDuration = 30; // days
int daysElapsed = (int)(DateTime.Now - installationDate).TotalDays;

if (daysElapsed >= trialPeriodDuration)
{
    // Disable functionality or prompt user to purchase license
}

Note that this is just a simple example and may need to be adapted to fit the specific needs of your software.

